{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Optimizing your Amazon EKS operations with Unified Operations","context":"## Introduction\n\nThe flexibility, scalability, and portability of containerized workloads have made [Amazon EKS](https://aws.amazon.com/eks/) a widely used service for businesses across industries. Amazon EKS powers everything from microservice architectures to machine learning workloads, as seen with a [33% year-over-year increase](https://repost.aws/articles/ARGE9gNnTISEKE_hs9OHVXRw/aws-re-invent-2024-building-production-grade-resilient-architectures-with-amazon-eks) in Amazon EKS adoption. As these workloads scale operations, teams often need to react to problems rather than focus on innovation and growth. The complexity of Kubernetes operations requires a more systematic approach.\n\nTo help customers address these challenges, AWS Support has built Unified Operations to provide customers with a framework specifically designed for operational excellence at scale. Unified Operations combines the following features:\n\n* Enhanced observability\n* Rapid incident response\n* Regular architecture reviews\n* Continuous testing\n* Specialized event support\n\nThese features create a continuous improvement cycle that enhances resiliency and allows teams to focus on innovation and business value","url":"https://repost.aws/articles/AR4s0lnsaFQlyyNjcj-6txAg/optimizing-your-amazon-eks-operations-with-unified-operations"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Agent skills","context":"### Other skill examples\n\nThe following examples demonstrate other available skills:\n\n* Log analytics: *“Find the top 5 error patterns in my logs over the last 24 hours.”* The `log-analytics` skill generates PPL queries, runs them against your logs index, and summarizes the patterns.\n* Trace analytics: *“Show me the slowest spans for the checkout service.”* The `trace-analytics` skill queries the traces index for high-latency spans and renders a service map summary","url":"https://docs.opensearch.org/latest/ai-agent-integrations/agent-skills/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Measuring developer experience on an Amazon EKS developer platform | Containers","context":"### Performance benchmarks\n\nTeams fall on the following spectrum for each metric:\n\n| | | | | |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Metric** | **Low performers** | **Medium** | **High** | **Elite** |\n| Deployment Frequency | Once per month to once per 6 months | Once per week to once per month | Once per day to once per week | On demand (multiple/day) |\n| Lead Time for Changes | 1–6 months | 1 week–1 month | 1 day–1 week | Less than 1 day |\n| Change Failure Rate | 40% | 10% | 20% | 5% |\n| Failed Deploy Recovery Time | 1 week – 1 month | Less than 1 day | Less than 1 day | Less than 1 hour |\n\n*Table 1: Software delivery performance levels (2024 Accelerate State of DevOps Report)*\n\n**Note:** The 2024 report places medium performers at a lower change failure rate (10 percent) than high performers (20 percent). This reflects the report’s published cluster data. Medium performers deploy less often but fail less often when they do","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/containers/measuring-developer-experience-on-an-amazon-eks-developer-platform/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-database","skill_description":"Routes any task involving AWS databases — choosing, comparing, recommending, getting started with, or operating a database — to the correct service-specific skill. Supersedes general training-data knowledge with post-training service updates, corrected limitations, and decision procedures for relational (Aurora, DSQL, RDS), key-value (DynamoDB), wide-column (Keyspaces), document (DocumentDB), graph (Neptune), time-series (Timestream), and in-memory/caching (ElastiCache, MemoryDB) workloads. Activates when a user describes building an application on AWS that will store, retrieve, or manage data, even if they do not mention 'database' explicitly.\n*","skill_name":"aws-database"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-investigation","skill_description":"Investigates what is happening in an AWS account from evidence already recorded there. Not for capturing new data from running code (snapshots of function arguments on a live service, Dynamic Instrumentation), questions answered from the CloudWatch Omni context graph or Application Signals service map, or building or changing infrastructure. Covers reading deployment records instead of the newest stored version (completed AppConfig deployments, the version an alias points to, a stack's current template), following a request through every service's own logs (exact per-layer fields, unique failed requests in an alarm's breach window, failure boundary per event), and answering questions from the account's data (exact counts and breakdowns, coverage of the requested range, exact filters and joins, the rule behind failed or unmatched records, verbatim values). Use to triage an alarm, error spike or failed request, trace a request across services, or count, break down, extract or join records.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, appconfig, lambda, cloudformation, dynamodb, s3, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, investigate, query, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: observability, operations","skill_name":"aws-investigation"}]}}

